\begin{abstract}
Why do citizens who accept unconstrained rule under adverse conditions later return to democratic institutions? I study \emph{undemocratic reversal}: a within-person shift from executive delegation back to constrained collective government after the structural justification for delegation disappears. The project uses a two-wave, incentivized laboratory panel conducted in synchronized sessions one week apart. In each wave, participants play ten rounds of a five-person institutional-choice public-good game. Before every allocation decision, each participant casts a secret ballot between a constrained collective procedure and an executive-delegation procedure; the majority choice binds the group. Under delegation, an elected executive can impose equal contributions and privately divert resources. Wave 1 gives delegation a productive advantage over ordinary institutions. In Wave 2, ten-person matching pools are randomly assigned either to a structural-recovery condition, which removes that advantage, or to a persistence condition, which retains it. Anonymous rematching, payoff-relevant votes, real distributive consequences, costly information, and repeated interaction make the design recognizably economic while preserving the original project's learning, memory, and process-tracing objectives. The primary outcome is whether a participant who chose delegation in Wave 1's final round chooses the constrained procedure in Wave 2's final round. Pre-information memory, costly search, belief updating, all twenty ballots, contributions, executive decisions, and realized payoffs reveal how that reversal occurs.
\end{abstract}

\clearpage
\pagenumbering{arabic}
\setcounter{page}{1}

\section{Research Question and Contribution}

Research on democratic backsliding shows that citizens sometimes trade institutional safeguards for policy congruence, material performance, or protection from an opposing camp \parencite{34047,7f36f,cd90d,0b3bf}. The harder question is dynamic: after citizens have learned that unconstrained authority is useful, what makes them abandon it? This proposal asks:

\begin{quote}
\emph{When the structural conditions that made executive discretion instrumentally attractive improve, do citizens return to constrained collective government, and through what learning and memory processes?}
\end{quote}

I call this behavioral transition \emph{undemocratic reversal}. It is not simply low support for an undemocratic alternative, nor a between-person difference in democratic attitudes. It is an observed within-person change: a participant initially endorses an institution that concentrates authority and weakens ex ante control, then later chooses an institution that restores collective choice and constraints. The concept therefore requires a panel structure and at least two consequential institutional choices by the same person.

The design contributes in three ways. First, it turns democratic constraint into a set of payoff-relevant rules governing interaction. Participants do not merely rate political descriptions. Their ballots select who controls endowments, how a public good is produced, and whether an executive may extract a private rent. This embeds democratic trade-offs in the classical experimental-economics traditions of public-good provision, delegation, voting, and rent extraction \parencite{Hamman2011}.

Second, it identifies a structural cause of reversal. All participants encounter low ordinary institutional capacity in Wave 1. Before Wave 2, matched pools are randomly assigned either to recovered capacity or to persistent incapacity. The executive institution itself does not improve or deteriorate. Consequently, the treatment changes the instrumental case for delegation rather than supplying a moral appeal to democracy.

Third, the experiment observes the mechanism rather than only its endpoint. Participants choose whether to buy information, state probabilistic beliefs before and after search, report what they remember after a one-week delay, vote in every round, and experience endogenous decisions by other subjects. The resulting record distinguishes durable learning from information retrieval, strategic adaptation, and repeated-game dynamics.

\section{Theory}

\subsection{Conditional tolerance of unconstrained authority}

Citizens need not reject democratic constraints in principle to support an unconstrained executive in practice. They may instead accept discretion conditionally, when ordinary institutions appear incapable of producing valued outcomes. This logic is compatible with evidence that electoral and material considerations can outweigh checks on executives and that citizens interpret democratic violations through partisan and performance-based trade-offs \parencite{7f36f,0b3bf,33fbf}. It also clarifies why democratic tolerance can be learned: repeated evidence of institutional failure changes the expected payoff from retaining constraints.

In the experiment, the constrained collective procedure (\Dproc) protects individual control. Each member chooses a contribution, benefits are shared equally, and no single person can appropriate the public account. Yet its low Wave-1 productivity makes underprovision costly. The executive-delegation procedure (\Aproc) solves the coordination problem by allowing one person to impose equal contributions and by supplying a more productive implementation technology. It also permits that executive to divert part of the account. A participant can therefore support \Aproc{} without expressing a general taste for autocracy: under weak ordinary capacity, delegation can be a rational response to institutional performance.

\paragraph{H1: Crisis endorsement.} In Wave 1, participants will increasingly support executive delegation when their experience indicates that its productive advantage outweighs the risks of executive extraction.

\subsection{Structural updating and democratic reversal}

The central theoretical claim is conditional. If support for unconstrained authority was learned from structural performance, then credible structural improvement should alter it. The Wave-2 recovery treatment raises the constrained procedure's productivity to the executive procedure's level. Recovery removes the executive's technological advantage but leaves its discretion to extract rents unchanged. A participant who understands this change should update the relative value of the two institutions.

The persistence condition supplies the counterfactual. Participants return after the same delay, complete the same memory task, face the same information prices, and play the same number of new rounds; only ordinary institutional capacity differs. A treatment-control contrast therefore separates recovery-induced change from generic maturation, regression to the mean, fatigue, or simply playing for a second day.

\paragraph{H2: Structural updating.} Participants assigned to recovery will report a larger increase in perceived ordinary institutional capacity and a larger reduction in the perceived instrumental need for delegation than participants assigned to persistence.

\paragraph{H3: Democratic reversal.} Among participants who choose \Aproc{} in Wave 1's final round, assignment to recovery will increase the probability of choosing \Dproc{} in Wave 2's final round.

\paragraph{H4: Behavioral mechanism.} Recovery will change the path of institutional choice across Wave 2: support for \Dproc{} should rise as participants experience its restored productivity, while executive extraction should make continued delegation less attractive.

\subsection{Memory and costly information}

Structural change cannot affect choice if it is neither encoded nor retrieved. Political learning may rest on a durable running evaluation or on specific considerations accessible when a decision is made. A one-week gap creates a meaningful test: before Wave-2 information is displayed, participants freely recall the prior environment, recognize its capacity level, recall their own final ballot, and state a prior probability that capacity recovered.

Information is then costly. Every participant begins the panel with a 24-point information endowment. Opening a full report costs four points, and unspent points are paid at the end. Headlines are neutral and do not disclose treatment status. Search therefore reveals a consequential demand for evidence rather than costless clicking. The same six report topics appear in each condition; condition-specific facts appear only after purchase. Participants state the probability of recovery again after search.

Information acquisition is endogenous, not itself randomized. It is a process measure: treatment effects on the final outcome will be reported both overall and descriptively by search behavior, but search will not be treated as an exogenous mediator without additional assumptions.

\paragraph{H5: Information and memory.} More accurate Wave-1 memory and greater acquisition of diagnostic Wave-2 information will predict more accurate recovery beliefs and a stronger alignment between final institutional choice and the realized environment.

\section{Experimental Design}

\subsection{Overview}

The study consists of two synchronized laboratory visits scheduled seven days apart. Each visit is designed for 55--65 minutes. Participants remain in stable, ten-person matching pools across waves, and treatment is randomized at the pool level. Within each pool, the software anonymously rematches participants into two five-person groups before every paid round. Thus, participants interact repeatedly within a treatment-consistent local population but cannot build a reliable bilateral reputation.

