\subsection{Wave 1: acquiring undemocratic tolerance}

After consent, participants receive common instructions, complete baseline capacity and risk beliefs, and answer a seven-item democratic-constraint battery. They then complete an unpaid guided practice block covering the constrained contribution decision, executive taxation and transfer, the ballot, report prices, and random-round payment. Three mandatory comprehension questions must be answered correctly before the paid task begins.

Participants receive neutrally titled report cards about ordinary capacity, the budget and service environment, collective constraints, executive implementation, oversight, and the institutional trade-off. Opening a report reveals its full content and deducts four points from the information endowment. The Wave-1 reports establish a common low-capacity environment: ordinary provision has multiplier 1.50, whereas executive implementation has multiplier 2.50.

Participants then play ten paid rounds. Every round follows the same sequence: secret institutional ballot; majority rule; contribution decisions under \Dproc{} or executive decisions under \Aproc; simultaneous outcome feedback; rematching. After Round 10, capacity and risk beliefs and the constraint battery are measured again. The software records the final ballot, the executive-vote share in Rounds 8--10, and all round-level behavior. One Wave-1 round is randomly chosen for payment.

\subsection{Wave 2: memory, structural change, and reversal}

At the second visit, participants first complete the memory module without access to prior materials. The module contains free recall, recognition of Wave-1 capacity, recall of the participant's own final Wave-1 ballot, and a 0--100 prior probability that ordinary capacity has recovered.

The same matching pools are then exposed to their randomly assigned Wave-2 environment. Under \emph{recovery}, ordinary capacity rises from 1.50 to 2.50. Under \emph{persistence}, it remains 1.50. Executive productivity, executive authority, and the maximum transfer remain constant. Six neutrally titled reports cover the same topic structure in both conditions; only purchased report content reveals whether recovery occurred. Immediately after search, participants state a new recovery probability and a capacity assessment.

Participants play ten additional rounds under the Wave-2 parameters. The same ballot and payoff structure makes behavior directly comparable across waves. After the final round, the capacity and risk measures and constraint battery are repeated. The final Wave-2 ballot is the behavioral endpoint. One Wave-2 round is randomly paid, and the software adds the unspent information endowment, a 25-point second-visit completion bonus, the scored-belief bonus, and the participation payment.

\section{Learning Across Repeated Play: Design Safeguards}

Repeated play is an advantage only if procedural learning is not confused with the substantive learning of interest. The design therefore anticipates six implementation concerns.

\paragraph{Procedural learning.} Participants may initially misunderstand payoff rules and improve merely through practice. A guided unpaid block and mandatory comprehension checks move rule learning before the paid data. Round 1 remains available for diagnostics, while the preregistered endpoint uses Round 10 and the late-round mean uses Rounds 8--10.

\paragraph{Reputation and reciprocity.} Fixed five-person groups would allow personal histories to drive votes and contributions. Anonymous rematching within ten-person pools breaks stable bilateral relationships while keeping treatment exposure and synchronized interaction feasible. Participants never receive persistent identities.

\paragraph{Executive-role learning.} Subjects could support delegation because they expect to become executive. Executive assignment is balanced among the least-served eligible members, preventing a few participants from monopolizing the role. Analyses will nevertheless control descriptively for prior executive service and realized rents.

\paragraph{Endogenous institutional experience.} Groups that never elect one procedure receive less experience with it. Instructions and practice expose everyone to both payoff rules, costly reports describe both institutions, and realized experience is recorded. Treatment effects will be reported by prior exposure, but the primary intention-to-treat contrast will not condition on a post-treatment institutional path.

\paragraph{Wealth, fatigue, and end-game effects.} Only one randomly selected round per wave determines game earnings, so accumulated wealth from early rounds does not mechanically alter later budgets. Wave instructions explain that each round has an equal payment chance. The ten-round horizon is disclosed and identical across conditions. Page timers, decision times, and timeouts identify fatigue or end-game behavior.

\paragraph{Cross-wave comparability.} The institution labels, executive rules, endowments, sequence, pool structure, and number of rounds are held constant. The recovery treatment changes only ordinary productivity and the reports that accurately describe it. A persistence group identifies change caused by returning to the lab, memory decay, or additional play.

These safeguards do not attempt to eliminate all learning. The study requires participants to learn from consequences. They instead separate theoretically meaningful learning about institutional performance from avoidable learning about the interface or accidental partners.

\section{Outcomes, Process Measures, and Estimands}

Let $V_{iwr}\in\{\Dproc,\Aproc\}$ be participant $i$'s ballot in wave $w$ and round $r$. Define Wave-1 executive endorsement as
\begin{equation}
A_{i1}=\ind{V_{i,1,10}=\Aproc}.
\end{equation}
For the theoretically relevant subgroup with $A_{i1}=1$, democratic reversal is
\begin{equation}
R_i=\ind{V_{i,2,10}=\Dproc}.
\end{equation}
Let $T_g=1$ indicate that matching pool $g$ received structural recovery. The primary causal estimand is
\begin{equation}
\tau_R=\mathbb{E}[R_i(1)-R_i(0)\mid A_{i1}=1],
\end{equation}
estimated as an intention-to-treat contrast with standard errors clustered by the ten-person randomization pool. Because $A_{i1}$ is measured before treatment disclosure, defining this subgroup does not condition on a post-treatment variable. The primary model will include prespecified baseline covariates only for precision; an unadjusted difference in means will also be reported.

Three secondary behavioral outcomes describe robustness: (1) the change in executive-vote share from Wave-1 Rounds 8--10 to Wave-2 Rounds 8--10; (2) the full round-by-treatment trajectory; and (3) group-level selection of each institution. Contribution, imposed tax, private transfer, public-account size, and realized payoff explain the strategic environment in which ballots change.

Process outcomes occur at defined points in the sequence: baseline, post-Wave 1, Wave-2 pre-information memory, immediate post-search beliefs, and post-Wave 2. They include perceived capacity, perceived risk to constraints, the seven-item constraint index, free recall, recognition accuracy, own-vote recall, report openings and order, search time, information expenditure, and probabilistic recovery beliefs. The order of reports and battery items is randomized once per participant and then stored, preventing re-randomization on refresh.

Belief accuracy will be evaluated against assigned capacity status. Search is costly by design, but it remains a choice. Accordingly, any decomposition through search or belief change will be labeled exploratory unless supported by a preregistered causal mediation strategy. The clean confirmatory claim is the pool-randomized effect of recovery on reversal.

\section{Randomization, Sample, and Power}

The planned sample is 400 Wave-1 participants organized into 40 ten-person matching pools. Pools are assigned in approximately equal numbers to recovery and persistence before Wave 1 but treatment is concealed until Wave 2. With 10 percent attrition, the expected returning sample is approximately 360 participants. Recruitment should overbook each synchronized cohort and maintain a standby list because interaction requires complete five-person groups.

Power depends on three quantities that cannot responsibly be inferred from the twenty repeated decisions: the proportion endorsing \Aproc{} at the Wave-1 endpoint, the persistence-group reversal rate, and the intrapool correlation. A pilot will estimate these values, after which power will be evaluated by simulation at the pool level under the planned assignment and attrition process. Repeated rounds increase measurement reliability and identify trajectories; they do not turn 400 participants or 40 randomized pools into 8,000 independent observations.

Randomization code assigns whole ten-person pools and approximately balances the number of pools across conditions. Production sessions should contain a multiple of ten participants. If several laboratory rooms run concurrently, randomization and matching remain within the configured cohort so that participants never interact across inconsistent treatment states.

\section{Payments and Laboratory Operations}

At EUR 0.20 per point, the implemented parameters are designed to yield an expected total payment of roughly EUR 28--32, including the EUR 10 participation payment, depending on play and information purchases. Final budgeting will use pilot distributions rather than theoretical maxima. All payoff components are displayed separately at completion: selected Wave-1 round, selected Wave-2 round, unspent information points, completion bonus, scored-belief bonus, point total, performance payment, and participation payment.

Wave 1 and Wave 2 are run as synchronized sessions rather than as short individual drop-ins. The second visit is scheduled at the first visit and reminder messages are sent without revealing treatment. A meaningful second-visit completion bonus reduces attrition. Participant codes, not names, link the waves.

The operational protocol requires complete groups. Standby participants may enter only before the first paid round. Once play begins, no replacement subject inherits another participant's history. Every strategic decision has a 90-second limit. If a participant times out, the software records the timeout and applies a neutral, prespecified default so that group members can continue. The session log must document late arrival, browser failure, early departure, manual intervention, and payment exceptions. Exclusion rules should be preregistered and should never depend on treatment-consistent behavior.

Before launch, two pilots are required. A technical pilot should use at least ten simultaneous clients and both treatment branches to stress wait pages, rematching, refresh persistence, timeouts, and payment calculations. A behavioral pilot should estimate duration, comprehension, Wave-1 delegation frequency, information demand, executive extraction, attrition risk, and payoff distributions. Parameters should be frozen after that pilot and before confirmatory registration.

\section{Ethics and Interpretation}

The experiment exposes participants to a setting in which concentrated authority can be materially effective. That feature is necessary to study conditional tolerance, but it creates an obligation to avoid deception and moral labeling. Instructions therefore use neutral procedure labels, disclose all payoff rules, and accurately explain executive discretion. Treatment-specific reports are true descriptions of the assigned game environment.

Consent explains the two-visit structure, interaction, anonymity, payment rule, data linkage, and right to withdraw. The debrief explains the democratic interpretation only after all incentivized decisions, clarifies that individual choices are not diagnoses of personal authoritarianism, and provides researcher and ethics-board contacts. Data exports should replace participant labels with study IDs and store the linkage key separately.

The interpretation will remain narrow. Choosing executive delegation in a stylized public-good environment is evidence about behavior under institutional trade-offs, not a complete measure of democratic commitment. The strongest contribution comes from the randomized change in those trade-offs and the observed within-person return to constraints.

\section{Planned Deliverables and Decision Rule}

The confirmatory package will contain the theory, hypotheses, full instructions, treatment reports, oTree code, randomization procedure, exclusion rules, primary estimand, and pool-level power simulation. The principal conclusion will follow a simple rule: evidence for structural democratic reversal requires a positive, substantively meaningful, and statistically distinguishable recovery effect on $R_i$ among Wave-1 executive endorsers. Round-level trajectories, beliefs, memory, information search, and allocations will be used to assess whether the observed endpoint follows the proposed learning mechanism and to identify rival explanations.

This design preserves the original project's core ambition---observing people learn and then potentially unlearn tolerance for unconstrained authority---while giving that learning a strategic and distributive foundation. Subjects vote over rules that govern other subjects, experience the consequences of those rules, decide whether evidence is worth buying, and return one week later to make the choice again under an experimentally altered structure.

